  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nLate Thursday night and into Friday morning will feature a round\nof nocturnal showers and thunderstorms, particularly across parts\nof far eastern Oklahoma and into western Arkansas due to another \nmid level disturbance and a weak low level jet oriented toward\nthose areas. Potential for locally heavy rains will continue with\nprecipitable water values around 2 inches persisting. The\npotential for further development during the day Friday will shift\nsouth and east, with a brief dry period by Friday evening. The\nrainfall and residual cloud cover during the day will keep\ntemperatures much cooler Friday than what the area has experienced\nof late, although still a couple of degrees above seasonal normal\nhighs. \n\nFor the weekend, the upper level ridge looks to expand back over\nthe region, although before it does, there may be one last round\nof morning showers and thunderstorms Saturday morning due to a\nCentral Plains mid level wave. Forecast highs are somewhat\nuncertain for Saturday given this potential, with longer lasting\nrainfall and cloud cover likely to lead to lower temperatures than\ncurrently forecast. \n\nWell above normal temperatures will extend into the early part of\nthe upcoming work week, with potential for additional heat \nheadlines both over the weekend and next week. The middle part of\nnext week may bring another cooldown and rainfall, with another\ncold front approaching the area. Timing and strength of this front\nand even how far into the area it may move are uncertain but it\nbrings some glimmer of hope.",
